          "text": "{\"relation\":\"REFINED\",\"consensus_summary\":\"Symbolically, a harlot represents spiritual or covenant unfaithfulness—especially idolatry, apostasy, and corrupt alliances. Revelation’s great harlot is commonly identified with Rome or a broader apostate world system, although Jerusalem is a recognized minority alternative.\",\"book_summary\":\"A harlot is specifically a covenant city or people that remains outwardly covenantal while being unfaithful to God. In Revelation, the book identifies the great harlot/Babylon literally as Jerusalem rather than Rome.\",\"consensus_core_identification\":\"A people, city, or system characterized by spiritual unfaithfulness to God; Revelation’s precise historical referent remains disputed.\",\"book_core_identification\":\"An outwardly covenantal but unfaithful city or people, specifically Jerusalem in Revelation.\",\"core_relation\":\"BOOK_NARROWS\",\"extension_relation\":\"PARTIAL_RECLASSIFICATION\",\"boundary_disagreement\":\"The book restricts the symbol to entities retaining an outward covenant identity, thereby excluding at least some non-covenant empires, commercial powers, or generic worldly systems admitted by the broader consensus. It also resolves Revelation’s disputed referent as Jerusalem, reclassifying the great harlot away from the dominant Rome/world-system identification.\",\"material_contradiction\":\"There is no contradiction with the consensus’s central symbolism of covenant unfaithfulness, and Jerusalem is already a recognized minority interpretation. There is, however, a direct contradiction with the dominant specific identification of Revelation’s harlot as Rome or an apostate global system.\",\"rationale\":\"The book preserves the established symbolic center—covenant infidelity—but adds a material condition: the harlot must retain the outward form of covenant. Its Jerusalem identification is not novel because the consensus explicitly lists that interpretation, yet it changes the treatment of Revelation’s principal case. The result is a substantive narrowing and partial reclassification rather than replacement of the core meaning.\",\"citation_support\":\"PARTIAL\",\"strongest_objection\":\"The printed texts establish Jerusalem as a harlot precedent and call both figures a “great city,” but they do not by themselves prove that the great city of Revelation 11:8 is identical to Babylon in Revelation 17. Revelation 17:18’s rule over kings may fit imperial Rome more naturally, so the Jerusalem equation requires additional argument from the referenced chapter.\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}"
        }
      ],
